1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,080
 Polypropylene is the second most commonly produced plastic in the world,

2
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:07,120
 accounting for around 20% of global plastic production.

3
00:00:07,120 --> 00:00:09,760
 Polypropylene has similar properties to polyethylene,

4
00:00:09,760 --> 00:00:12,480
 but it's generally considered to be a little bit more hardy,

5
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:15,760
 a little bit stronger, a little bit stiffer, a little bit more heat resistant.

6
00:00:15,760 --> 00:00:19,760
 And the reason that polypropylene and polyethylene have reasonably similar properties

7
00:00:19,760 --> 00:00:22,000
 is that they have very similar monomers.

8
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:24,960
 A propylene monomer looks an awful lot like an ethylene monomer,

9
00:00:24,960 --> 00:00:27,680
 it just has one of the hydrogens replaced with a methyl.

10
00:00:27,680 --> 00:00:31,440
 Because polypropylene offers slightly more robust mechanical properties compared

11
00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:36,720
 to polyethylene at around the same price, because the same economies of scale apply here.

12
00:00:36,720 --> 00:00:39,600
 It's normally used in applications that require a little bit more grunt,

13
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:43,040
 industrial packaging, car bumper bars, bottle tops, that kind of thing.

14
00:00:43,040 --> 00:00:46,720
 Now, while polypropylene can be recycled, it is a thermoplastic,

15
00:00:46,720 --> 00:00:49,520
 nearly 99% of it ends up in landfill.

16
00:00:49,520 --> 00:00:54,080
 This is because most recycling plants are set up to only recycled polyethylene.

17
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:56,640
 As far as recycling of polyethylene is concerned,

18
00:00:56,720 --> 00:00:58,960
 introduction of polypropylene is a contaminant.

19
00:00:58,960 --> 00:01:03,200
 Because of the risk of contamination of polypropylene in your polyethylene recycling,

20
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:07,680
 usually at the point of recycling, most polypropylene is sent straight to landfill.

21
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:11,600
 Because it's not economical to then send it somewhere else to be recycled as polypropylene.

22
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:15,200
 This is why soft drinks ask you to remove the label and the bottle top before you put them

23
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:17,920
 in your recycling bin and to put those things into your red bin.

24
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:19,920
 It's because they'd be headed for landfill anyway,

25
00:01:19,920 --> 00:01:24,160
 and at least you can save some of the energy in the CO2 emissions by sending them straight

26
00:01:24,160 --> 00:01:34,160
 there instead of sending them to the recycling plant and then off to landfill afterwards.
